  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nAs of 230AM Friday morning, an omega blocking pattern was present \nacross the CONUS with trough axis' across the coasts and ridging \nacross the central US. A weak perturbation was lifting across the \nFour Corners region with scattered showers and thunderstorms across \nNM/CO. The overall pattern is not progged to shift much over the \nnext few days. With the forecast area remaining under the influence \nof the midlevel ridge, temperatures will remain above normal through \nthe weekend with highs in the upper 70s to near 80. In addition, \nthis will keep the best rain chances well-west of the forecast area \nand across far western KS and CO. By late Saturday night into \nSunday, a strong northern stream midlevel trough will slide across \nthe southern Canadian provinces, deamplifying the ridge axis across \nthe MS Valley. As the ridge deamplifies, the western CONUS trough \nwill eventually eject into the Plains early next week. It's arrival \nwill bring rain chances back to the area and temperatures near-\nnormal. A secondary shortwave trough across the Pacific NW may \nemerge across the central Plains towards the middle of next week, \nbringing additional rain chances to the forecast area Wednesday. \nNorthwest midlevel looks to build in for the second half of next \nweek yielding dry and seasonable temperatures in the upper 60s and \nlower 70s.",
